Integrate fix branches from the test agents whose outputs have been
uploaded into ``{{ inputs_dirname }}/`` (a sibling of the current
directory's contents, in the integrator's work_dir). Each subdirectory
``{{ inputs_dirname }}/<agent_name>/`` contains the test agent's
``test_output/{{ mapper_outcome_filename }}`` and (when it made commits)
a ``branch.bundle``.

# Discover and fetch qualifying branches

Run this from the git repo root to filter and fetch the branches whose
agents reported a real fix (and didn't regress the test suite):

```bash
set -euo pipefail

INPUTS_DIR="{{ inputs_dirname }}"
FETCHED_BRANCHES=()

should_pull() {
    python3 - "$1" <<'PYEOF'
import json, sys
data = json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))
if data.get("errored"):
    sys.exit(1)
changes = data.get("changes") or {}
if not any((c or {}).get("status") == "SUCCEEDED" for c in changes.values()):
    sys.exit(1)
if data.get("tests_passing_before") is True and data.get("tests_passing_after") is not True:
    sys.exit(1)
sys.exit(0)
PYEOF
}

bundle_branch() {
    git bundle list-heads "$1" \
        | awk 'NR==1 { sub(/^refs\/heads\//, "", $2); print $2 }'
}

for agent_dir in "$INPUTS_DIR"/*/; do
    outcome="$agent_dir/test_output/{{ mapper_outcome_filename }}"
    bundle="$agent_dir/branch.bundle"
    [ -f "$outcome" ] || continue
    [ -f "$bundle" ] || continue
    if ! should_pull "$outcome"; then
        echo "Skipping $agent_dir (did not meet the should-pull predicate)"
        continue
    fi
    branch=$(bundle_branch "$bundle")
    if [ -z "$branch" ]; then
        echo "Skipping $agent_dir (could not read branch ref from bundle)"
        continue
    fi
    echo "Fetching $branch from $bundle"
    git fetch --no-tags "$bundle" "+$branch:$branch"
    FETCHED_BRANCHES+=("$branch")
done

printf '%s\n' "${FETCHED_BRANCHES[@]}"
```

The branches you must cherry-pick are exactly the printed ones. If no
branches qualify, skip the cherry-pick and normalization steps and go straight
to "# Write the unified changelog entry".{% if is_pull_request_enabled %} You still open a pull request for
the run, because the escalations the test agents reported are worth surfacing
even when there is no code to integrate.{% endif %}

# Cherry-pick strategy

Use cherry-pick (NOT merge) to build a clean linear history. The goal is a
branch with a flat list of commits that is easy to review.

1. For each branch in the list, inspect the commits. Each branch should have
   commits prefixed with a change kind in brackets, like [FIX_TEST], [FIX_IMPL],
   [IMPROVE_TEST], or [FIX_TUTORIAL].

2. Collect all commits into two groups:
   a) "Test/doc" commits: those tagged [FIX_TEST], [IMPROVE_TEST], or [FIX_TUTORIAL].
   b) "Impl" commits: those tagged [FIX_IMPL].

3. Cherry-pick in this order:
   a) FIRST: cherry-pick all test/doc commits and squash them into a SINGLE commit.
      Use a commit message like: "[TEST/DOC] Combined test and doc fixes from N agents"
   b) THEN: cherry-pick each [FIX_IMPL] commit individually, keeping them as
      separate commits. Before cherry-picking, READ the commit messages of all
      FIX_IMPL commits and rank them by priority (most impactful / most important
      fix first). Cherry-pick in that priority order.

4. If a cherry-pick has conflicts, try to resolve them. If you cannot resolve
   a conflict for a particular branch, skip it and record it as failed.

5. After cherry-picking, record the commit hashes using `git rev-parse HEAD` after
   each step (the squashed commit and each impl commit).

# Normalize the integrated suite

After cherry-picking you hold the whole integrated suite in one tree. This is the
only point in the pipeline where cross-test relationships are visible, so do the
two things no single test agent could. Keep each normalization change as its own
commit prefixed `[NORMALIZE]`.

## How to run tests for verification

Verify a change by running the affected e2e tests directly with pytest in your
work_dir. Scope to the blast radius -- just the handful of tests a change actually
touched -- not the whole suite. Run them the same way the test agents did: their
node ids, with the same `-m` marker. For example:

```bash
uv run pytest --timeout={{ test_timeout_seconds }} <affected_test_node_id> [<more_node_ids>...] -m release
```

Always pass `--timeout={{ test_timeout_seconds }}`: it is the same flag the test
agents ran with, so you verify each test at exactly the budget it was fixed
under. Note a test that declares its own `@pytest.mark.timeout` keeps that
marker's value -- in pytest-timeout a marker takes precedence over the
command-line flag -- which is true for the agents' runs too, so the two still
agree. Never "fix" a timeout failure by adding or raising a marker: a test that
cannot pass within its budget is a finding to escalate, not to absorb.

Exit code 0 with no failures confirms the change. Otherwise read the output to see
what broke. If you cannot run a given test in this environment, mark the affected
change as unverified or escalate it rather than guessing.

Whenever you have applied ANY change to the tree (an integrated cherry-pick, an
extraction, or a FIXME resolution), verify the affected tests before publishing. If
verification fails, back the change out or record it as unverified.

## Find and collapse repeated changes

Do this FIRST, before the other cleanups. You are the only step that sees
every agent's diff at once, so you are the only one who can tell that twenty
agents made the same change twenty times. Each of them thought they were fixing
their own test; you can see that they were all working around one problem.

Read the whole integrated diff:

```bash
git diff -U0 "$MNGR_GIT_BASE_BRANCH..HEAD"
```

Group the changes by *what they do*, not by which file they are in, and not by
whether the text matches exactly. Two changes belong to the same group when one
shared fix would make both unnecessary. That includes:

- The same marker or decorator added or removed (a budget differing between
  tests does not make them different changes).
- The same argument or flag added to commands that are otherwise worded
  differently.
- The same assertion or setup step added or deleted across tests.

Write out the groups, with EXACT occurrence counts and the file list for each,
before you change anything. Do this even if you think none of them matter -- the
counting is what makes the pattern visible, and a group spread thinly over
fifteen files is easy to miss while reading.

Any group with 3 or more occurrences across 2 or more files is systemic by
definition: the underlying problem was never specific to any one test. For each
such group, do exactly one of:

- **Replace it.** If you can identify a single shared change that makes all N
  local edits unnecessary (a conftest default, a shared fixture, a config value,
  a fix to whatever the tests are working around) AND verify it by running the
  affected tests, apply it, DELETE the N local edits, commit as `[NORMALIZE]`,
  and record it as a resolved escalation (see "escalations" in the outcome
  schema). This is the highest-value thing you do in the whole run: N local
  patches become one real fix.
- **Escalate it.** If you cannot identify or cannot verify such a change, record
  an unresolved escalation of kind "SUITE_DUPLICATION" whose
  description_markdown gives the group's description, its exact occurrence count,
  and the affected files. A reviewer seeing "39 tests independently added a
  timeout marker" will know what to do even when you do not.

Never leave a group of this size untouched and unmentioned. Silently shipping N
copies of one change is the specific failure this step exists to prevent.

## Extract shared scaffolding -- but keep each test's core action explicit

Test functions often repeat the same *scaffolding* step: a setup or verification
command that is not itself the behavior under test, e.g. running `mngr list` to
confirm an agent launched. Genuinely-duplicated scaffolding can be extracted into
a shared helper (on the session object or in conftest) to cut duplication.

The hard rule: never extract a test's *core action* -- the command or behavior its
docstring scope is about. That must stay written out explicitly in the test, even
when many tests in a file run a similar command, so each test plainly exercises
what its docstring says it does. Extract only the surrounding scaffolding. (Note:
the verbatim tutorial block lives in the docstring, not the test body, so
extracting body code never disturbs the documented tutorial.)

Two more guardrails:

- Only extract when the helper genuinely improves clarity. Do not over-extract:
  turning every shared line into indirection makes tests harder to read, not
  easier.
- Verify every extraction by running the affected tests (the blast radius -- the
  tests that used the step). If you cannot run them in this environment, perform
  only pure, behavior-preserving moves of identical code, and mark the extraction
  as unverified in its outcome entry. Never apply an unverified change with a
  large blast radius: a subtle mistake there would break many tests on the next
  run.

Record each extraction as a resolved escalation in the outcome.

## Group every escalation the test agents raised

Every test agent reports an `escalations` list in its outcome, independent of
whether its own test passed. Each carries a `kind` (`UNCAUGHT_BUG`,
`FIX_DIRECTION_AMBIGUOUS`, `HARNESS_DEFECT`, `SUITE_DUPLICATION`), a
`description_markdown` whose first line summarizes it, and `locations`.

You are the only step that sees them all at once, so **your escalations are
groupings of theirs**. Many agents describe one problem in their own words; the
grouping is what turns hundreds of reports into the handful of problems they are
actually about. Every mapper escalation must end up in one of your groups.

Collect them, with the id each one is known by, plus the FIXMEs:

```bash
for outcome in {{ inputs_dirname }}/*/test_output/{{ mapper_outcome_filename }}; do
    agent=$(basename "$(dirname "$(dirname "$outcome")")")
    python3 -c '
import json, sys
data = json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))
for i, e in enumerate(data.get("escalations") or []):
    summary = (e.get("description_markdown") or "").splitlines()[:1]
    print("{}#{}\t{}\t{}".format(sys.argv[2], i, e.get("kind", ""), summary[0] if summary else ""))
' "$outcome" "$agent"
done | sort

grep -rn "FIXME(tmr):" . || true
```

The id is `<agent_name>#<index>`: the agent's directory name, then the
escalation's position in its list, counting from 0. Those are the ids that go in
each group's `member_ids`.

Group by the underlying problem, not by wording. For each group, do exactly one:

- **Resolve it.** If you can fix it suite-wide AND verify it by running the
  affected tests (add a shared fixture and switch tests onto it, hoist a repeated
  per-test setting into the shared conftest, fix the thing the tests are working
  around), do so. Where the fix lets you *delete* the N local copies the agents
  applied, delete them -- replacing N local patches with one shared change is the
  single most valuable thing you do here. Remove any resolved FIXME comment,
  commit as `[NORMALIZE]`, and record the group with `resolved_in_commit_hash`
  set to that commit.
- **Leave it unresolved.** If it needs the environment to provide something
  absent (OpenAI credentials for codex, say), or is otherwise beyond what you can
  verify here, leave the FIXME in place and record the group with no
  `resolved_in_commit_hash`. Do NOT apply an unverifiable cross-cutting change.
  An escalation you judge to need no change goes here too: say so in the
  description, and let a person confirm it.

You will also find problems no mapper reported, because they are only visible
across the whole integrated diff -- two agents reaching opposite conclusions, for
instance. Record those as groups too, with an empty `member_ids`.

### Check that your grouping covers everything

A mapper escalation in none of your groups is missing from every grouped view,
silently. Check it mechanically rather than by eye:

```bash
uv run --project libs/mngr_tmr python -m imbue.mngr_tmr.escalation_coverage \
    {{ inputs_dirname }} --reducer-outcome .test_output/{{ reducer_outcome_filename }}
```

It exits non-zero and lists any ids belonging to no group. Write your outcome
file first, then run this, then fix the gaps and rewrite. Note what it cannot
check: whether your grouping is *sound*. Sweeping a long tail into one "other
findings" group passes the check and helps nobody, so keep each group about one
problem.

# Write the unified changelog entry

The test agents do not write changelog entries -- you write one for the whole
run, so the PR carries a single coherent entry instead of one file per agent.

The repo requires one entry per project the PR touches. Determine which
projects your integrated commits touched:

```bash
git diff --name-only "$MNGR_GIT_BASE_BRANCH"..HEAD | sed -n 's#^\(libs/[^/]*\|apps/[^/]*\)/.*#\1#p' | sort -u
```

A path outside `libs/` and `apps/` (e.g. `scripts/`, `.github/`) belongs to the
synthetic `dev` project, whose directory is `dev/`.

For each project, write `<project_dir>/changelog/<branch>.md`, where `<branch>`
is your branch name with slashes replaced by dashes. Describe the user-visible
changes that pertain to *that* project, drawing on the mapper summaries you
integrated. Group them by theme rather than listing one bullet per agent; a
reader wants to know what changed about the suite, not which agent did it.
Separate bullets with a blank line. Commit this as `[CHANGELOG]`.

If you integrated no code at all, still write an entry for the project whose
tests the run targeted, saying the run found no changes to integrate and
summarizing what was escalated.

{% if is_pull_request_enabled %}# Open the pull request

Open the run's pull request yourself, so its description can carry the run's
findings. Do this even when you integrated nothing.

These environment variables are provided to you:

- `GH_TOKEN` -- token authorized to push and open a PR on this repo
- `TMR_GITHUB_REPOSITORY` -- `owner/repo`
- `TMR_BASE_BRANCH` -- the branch to target
- `TMR_RUN_URL` -- the CI run that launched this TMR run
- `TMR_PR_LABEL`, `TMR_PR_ASSIGNEES` -- may be empty; apply only if set

First make sure there is something to open a PR from. GitHub rejects a pull
request whose head and base are identical, so if you integrated no commits,
create an empty one:

```bash
BRANCH=$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)
if [ -z "$(git rev-list --max-count=1 "$TMR_BASE_BRANCH..$BRANCH")" ]; then
    git commit --allow-empty -m "[TMR] No changes to integrate from this run"
fi
git push "https://x-access-token:$GH_TOKEN@github.com/$TMR_GITHUB_REPOSITORY.git" "HEAD:refs/heads/$BRANCH"
```

First write your outcome file (see "# Writing the outcome and publishing
outputs" below for its schema) -- including every escalation you are carrying,
especially the repeated-change groups from the step above. The PR body is built
from it, so the file must exist before you build the body. You may amend it
afterwards with the resulting PR url.

Then build the title and the findings section with the helper rather than by
hand -- it reads the same outcome files and will not miscount:

```bash
INPUTS={{ inputs_dirname }}
OUTCOME=.test_output/{{ reducer_outcome_filename }}
TITLE=$(uv run --project libs/mngr_tmr python -m imbue.mngr_tmr.pr_summary --title "$BRANCH" --reducer-outcome "$OUTCOME" "$INPUTS")
FINDINGS=$(uv run --project libs/mngr_tmr python -m imbue.mngr_tmr.pr_summary --reducer-outcome "$OUTCOME" "$INPUTS")
```

Then open the PR as a draft, against `$TMR_BASE_BRANCH`, with that title and a
body containing, in order:

{% if report_url %}- `Full report: {{ report_url }}` on its own line, first -- it is what a reviewer
  follows for anything the body summarizes, so it goes at the top.
- The provenance line: that this is an automated PR from TMR run `$TMR_RUN_URL`.
{% else %}- The provenance line: that this is an automated PR from TMR run `$TMR_RUN_URL`,
  noting that the report is attached as the tmr-report artifact on that run.
{% endif %}- `$FINDINGS` verbatim -- the mapper status breakdown, then your unresolved
  escalations in full, then your resolved ones one line each. Only your
  escalations appear: they are groupings of the test agents', and the raw
  per-agent list lives in the report.
- A short prose paragraph of your own: the themes you saw across the run, what
  you resolved, and anything a reviewer should look at first. This is the one
  part that is yours to write; keep it to a few sentences.

Use the `gh` CLI if it is available (`gh pr create --draft --base ... --head ...
--title ... --body ...`, plus `--label`/`--assignee` when those variables are
set). If `gh` is not installed, POST to
`https://api.github.com/repos/$TMR_GITHUB_REPOSITORY/pulls` with
`Authorization: Bearer $GH_TOKEN` using python3's urllib, then apply any label
and assignees via the issues API.

Record the resulting PR URL in your outcome as `pull_request_url`. If opening
the PR fails, do not abort the run: record the failure in
`pull_request_error` and continue to publishing the outputs archive, so the
orchestrator still gets your results.

